1682,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Muahahaha.\",,1683,
1683,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"You've done it now.\",,1684,
1684,,Summer,,\"What?\",,1685,
1685,,Summer,,\"How did this radio turn on?\",,1686,
1686,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"You turned it on, Summer.\",,1687,
1687,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"You're using\\n…\\nunnatural powers.\",,1688,
1688,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"It's these unnatural powers that unlocked my spirit within this radio.\",,1689,
1689,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Did you think no one would notice?\",,1690,
1690,,Summer,,\"What's happening! How do you know my name?\",,1691,
1691,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Oh Summer, I know a great many things. I know when someone is trying to skip past the order of destined things.\",,1692,
1692,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Skip is my name after all.\",,1693,
1693,,Summer,,\"What do you mean?\",,1694,
1694,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"I am a ghost that was put into this radio to keep the flow of time aligned.\",,1695,
1695,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"You tried to bypass that alignment and that is what has awakened me.\",,1696,
1696,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"You shouldn't be here right now and yet you are.\",,1697,
1697,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"I bet you wish you could skip past this and get back to your day but you're scared stiff.\",,1698,
1698,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Unable to move.\\nLocked in this conversation with me.\",,1699,
1699,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Now that I have you as a captive audience; let me tell you a story.\",,1700,
1700,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"In 1854, an expedition headed by businessman Newton Lancaster landed at Pine Cove.\",,1701,
1701,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"The beach was littered with driftwood, flotsam, and jetsam.\",,1702,
1702,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Lancaster and his crew spent months clearing the debris and wreckage.\",,1703,
1703,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"They used the driftwood to built a small settlement of shacks and a dock.\",,1704,
1704,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Newton Lancaster wished for the settlement to be named after himself but the settlement took on the colloquial name of Driftwood Town.\",,1705,
1705,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Then gold was discovered.\",,1706,
1706,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"The area along Lancaster River brought riches to the inhabitants of Driftwood Town for the better part of three decades.\",,1707,
1707,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"When the gold ran out; the town transitioned into a lumber mill town.\",,1708,
1708,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"After that it became a supply point for those traveling to the Klondike.\",,1709,
1709,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"In 1904 the town was incorporated simply as Driftwood.\",,1710,
1710,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"That same year, Newton Lancaster died from complications stemming from an old gunshot wound he won during a gambling dispute in Cody Joe’s Saloon.\",,1711,
1711,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"To honor the founder of the town that didn’t take his name; Driftwood started the Newt Festival.\",,1712,
1712,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"As the years went by, the festival drifted away from its original meaning.\",,1713,
1713,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Now the Newt Festival celebrates the local population of rough-skinned newts.\",,1714,
1714,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Well then.\",,1715,
1715,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"I think that is enough of a history lesson.\",,1716,
1716,,Skip the Radio Ghost,,\"Goodbye for now. \",,END,
